n saved successfully.\\\"). Navigated back to Models list and reopened the Software Engineer model. DOM inspection of .react-flow__node transforms showed the Agile node had reverted to its original default position translate(600px, 0px), not the saved/dragged position. All other node positions were also at defaults. The saved state was not actually restored.\", \"evidence\": \"browser_evaluate on reopened model: {\\\"prog-lang\\\":\\\"translate(0px, 0px)\\\",\\\"sys-design\\\":\\\"translate(0px, 200px)\\\",\\\"code-review\\\":\\\"translate(0px, 400px)\\\",\\\"problem-solve\\\":\\\"translate(300px, 0px)\\\",\\\"collab\\\":\\\"translate(300px, 200px)\\\",\\\"agile\\\":\\\"translate(600px, 0px)\\\"} — agile should have been translate(253.423px, 201.15px) if persisted.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"CS-12\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Level and Weight bound enforcement cannot be verified because the editing controls themselves are non-functional\", \"actual\": \"Attempted to change a competency's Level via the 5 Level buttons (1-5) on the Programming Languages node (starting at Level \\\"Advanced\\\"). Clicked Level button 1 (leftmost) and Level button 2 — both showed [active] pseudo-state on click but the node's Level label remained \\\"Advanced\\\" (verified via card.innerText both before and after each click). The Weight slider (role=slider, aria-valuemin=10, aria-valuemax=100) was already confirmed non-responsive in FT-4 (no change via keyboard, drag, or fill_form). Since neither the Level control nor the Weight control actually changes the underlying value, it is impossible to test whether the app enforces the 1-5 level bound or the 10%-100% weight bound (e.g., clamping at boundaries) — the constraint cannot be exercised at all.\", \"evidence\": \"card.innerText before and after clicking Level buttons 1 and 2 both returned: \\\"Programming Languages\\\\n\\\\nProficiency in multiple programming languages\\\\n\\\\nTechnical\\\\nCritical\\\\nLevel\\\\nAdvanced\\\\nWeight\\\\n90%\\\" — unchanged despite [active] click states registering on the buttons.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"IX-13\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Coverage recompute is not reliably tied to actual competency edits; fires incorrectly on node selection instead\", \"actual\": \"Clicked the enable/disable switch on the Programming Languages node. The switch's own aria-checked/visual state did NOT change (stayed checked=true, confirming the switch itself is non-functional per FT-5). However, Coverage instantly changed from 68% to 65% and Critical Path count changed from 3 to 5 (Problem Solving, System Design, Programming Languages +2) as a side effect of the click — even though no actual competency was disabled and no genuine edit took effect. This shows the \\\"real-time\\\" recompute is triggered by node selection/click, not by validated model edits, producing numerically arbitrary/incorrect metric changes disconnected from any real state change. Separately, adjusting the Scenario Simulation category sliders (Level Change) did NOT update Coverage/Avg Level in real time (unchanged at 68%/3.3) until Run Simulation was clicked, and this same interaction corrupted the paired Weight Change field to display \\\"NaN%\\\".\", \"evidence\": \"Before switch click: Coverage 68%, Critical Path 3 (Programming Languages, Problem Solving, System Design). After switch click (switch still shows checked=true): Coverage 65%, Critical Path 5 (Problem Solving, System Design, Programming Languages, +2). Category Level Change slider showed \\\"+1\\\" after ArrowRight but paired Weight Change field showed \\\"NaN%\\\".\"}, {\"item_id\": \"IX-19\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"On every fresh navigation into a job model's detail page (verified across multiple reload+reopen cycles for Software Engineer), the Scenario Simulation panel consistently initializes with the \\\\\\\"Recruitment\\\\\\\" tab selected by default (aria-selected=true) and all Category Adjustments (Level Change / Weight Change for technical, behavioral, leadership, domain) reset to their neutral defaults (0 / 0%), giving a consistent, predictable starting state.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"IX-20\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Tested the graph's \\\"toggle interactivity\\\" control for consistency with both graph behavior and the summary analysis panel. (1) Clicking \\\"toggle interactivity\\\" (OFF) caused the button to show an [active] visual state, and a subsequent drag attempt on the Agile Methodology node failed with Playwright reporting \\\"react-flow__pane intercepts pointer events\\\" — the node's transform remained unchanged at translate(600px, 0px), confirming dragging was correctly disabled. (2) Clicking \\\"toggle interactivity\\\" again (ON) restored normal behavior: dragging the Agile node onto the Collaboration node successfully updated its transform to translate(253.423px, 201.15px). (3) Throughout both toggle states and the drag interactions, the summary analysis panel (Coverage 68%, Critical Path 3: Programming Languages, Problem Solving, System Design) remained accurate and unchanged, since no competency data was altered by these graph-control-only interactions — confirming the graph control state (interactivity on/off) stays consistent with, and does not corrupt, the summary analysis.\"}, {\"item_id\": \"CT-15\", \"verdict\": \"PASS\", \"issue\": null, \"actual\": null, \"evidence\": \"Graph renders as an interactive directed weighted graph via React Flow. Zoom in button changed viewport transform scale from 0.735768 to 0.882922; Fit View button changed transform to translate(37.3636px, 61.4138px) scale(0.802656) — confirming functional zoom/pan/fit controls. Edges are rendered with percentage weight labels (80%, 70%, 90%, 60%, 50%) and accessible names like \\\"Edge from prog-lang to code-review\\\". SVG <marker> elements with type=arrowclosed (3 distinct colors matching edge/category colors) confirm directional arrowheads are rendered on edges. Node dragging was also demonstrated to work visually within a session (Agile node moved from default position to translate(253.423px, 201.15px) via drag, per FT-11 testing), confirming nodes can be rearranged on the canvas even though the new position does not persist after save (separately tracked as FT-11 FAIL).\"}, {\"item_id\": \"CT-16\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Critical-path/coverage recalculation produces corrupted (NaN) output after running a simulation with a Level Change adjustment\", \"actual\": \"Set the \\\"technical\\\" category Level Change slider to -2 in the Recruitment simulation tab (via keyboard ArrowLeft), which also corrupted the paired Weight Change field to display \\\"NaN%\\\" (a pre-existing bug also noted in IX-13). Clicked \\\"Run Simulation\\\". After running, the Critical Path count/members stayed the same (3: Programming Languages, System Design, Problem Solving) and the \\\"Critical\\\" badge highlighting on graph nodes remained consistent with the summary panel list. However, the Coverage metric in the summary panel changed to \\\"NaN%\\\" instead of a valid percentage — confirming that the recalculation triggered by Run Simulation propagates the corrupted Weight Change value into the model's derived metrics, producing an invalid, user-visible NaN result rather than a correct recalculation. This is a genuine functional defect in the recalculation pipeline that a user would observe directly in the Coverage card.\", \"evidence\": \"Post Run-Simulation evaluate result: {\\\"criticalPath\\\":\\\"Critical Path\\\\n3\\\\ncompetencies\\\\nProgramming Languages\\\\nSystem Design\\\\nProblem Solving...\\\",\\\"coverage\\\":\\\"Coverage\\\\nNaN%\\\\nof model...\\\",\\\"riskGaps\\\":\\\"Risk Gaps\\\\n0\\\\nidentified...\\\"}\"}, {\"item_id\": \"CT-17\", \"verdict\": \"FAIL\", \"issue\": \"Risk Gaps warning never triggers even under conditions that should clearly represen